# After gateup-occ5.card (decide-below): the gate.up gemv's warps wait on DRAM (long_scoreboard 99.6 %), so the lever
# is bytes in flight per SM, not warps. q4k_row_dot_1col_pair walks the gate and up rows together, each iteration's
# loads for both issued before either row's multiply-add; each row's sum keeps its order (bit for bit). ptx-scan: 80
# registers, no spill, 3 blocks a SM (24 warps, was 32 at 59), ld.global scalar 103 (was 133: the column's q, s8, d8
# loads are shared by the two rows). Chains in flight per SM 24 x 2 = 48 against 32: x1.5. If that lifts gate.up
# from 81.7 % of the bus toward the head's 93.4 %: 68.37 -> 59.8 us x 64 = -548 us of 11.88 ms = +4.6 % at the top
# [derived]; half of it is the band's floor.
kind: ab
question: does the paired gate.up walk speed a full-card decode step?
term: decode tok/s @ depth 512, Qwen3.8-27B Q4_K_M whole on the RTX 5090, generate_qwen3moe of the gupair tree against
  main 35fa1c195, (gupair - main) / main
unit: %
predict: +1.0..+4.6
rounds: 6
sd: 0.05 % docs/cards/nqfold-split.card and shortk-ba-fold.card outcomes, paired round-ratio SD 0.014 % and 0.017 % over 6 rounds each, rounded up threefold
condition: just depth-gpu-qwen35moe with both arms at 512@model=/models/Qwen3.8-27B/Qwen3.8-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf in one
  lease, the base as a bin: arm; the 5090 alone at 600 W
decide-in: land it with gate-gpu-qwen35moe-e2e and the expert gates bit-identical; try the same pairing on qkv
decide-below: under +1.0: the shared activation loads, not the chains, were the gain; read ncu before going on
